1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Human civilization has come a long way since the time of the ancient Greeks and Romans.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Yet if we could step back 2,000 years into the world of the Roman Empire, we'd be in for a great surprise.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Incredibly, ancient doctors carried out brain surgery, eye operations, and even cosmetic procedures.

4
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Next on Modern Marvels, Ancient Discoveries 2.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Incredible discoveries by archaeologists and historians have shown that complex medical

6
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:49,460
procedures were in everyday use in Roman times.

7
00:00:49,461 --> 00:00:58,140
Skills that have been lost for thousands of years and only recently been reinvented.

8
00:00:58,141 --> 00:01:03,980
In a time without electricity or modern drugs, it is amazing to realize that doctors in the

9
00:01:03,981 --> 00:01:10,520
ancient classical world carried out over 130 surgical procedures which are almost identical

10
00:01:10,521 --> 00:01:16,300
to those taught to medical students today.

11
00:01:16,301 --> 00:01:20,880
As more intriguing clues are uncovered, it is becoming clear that the Romans were able

12
00:01:20,881 --> 00:01:28,120
to carry out many forms of dangerous and difficult surgery.

13
00:01:28,121 --> 00:01:32,840
You may have been safer in the hands of doctors in the ancient world than you might think.

14
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:39,380
Roman medicine is extremely impressive.

15
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:44,080
If one looks at their methods of observation, if one looks at their diagnoses,

16
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:47,940
and if one looks at the sort of operations that they carried out,

17
00:01:47,941 --> 00:01:52,480
one can see that this was not surpassed for perhaps another 1,500 years.

18
00:01:52,481 --> 00:02:03,980
modern medicine has changed our lives dramatically just 200 years ago surgery was still a brutal and

19
00:02:03,981 --> 00:02:08,800
bloody lottery but today it has become a precise and successful science

20
00:02:08,801 --> 00:02:18,580
yet hidden within ancient texts lie secrets which reveal that many of our modern scientific

21
00:02:18,581 --> 00:02:25,037
discoveries were already known 2,000 years ago.

22
00:02:25,038 --> 00:02:30,120
We now know a Roman doctor would have

23
00:02:30,121 --> 00:02:35,020
recognized a modern set of medical instruments and would have been just as highly skilled

24
00:02:35,021 --> 00:02:40,488
and trained in their use as a doctor of today.

25
00:02:40,489 --> 00:02:45,600
We tend to think of scientific medicine and

26
00:02:45,601 --> 00:02:47,788
surgery as modern-day inventions.

27
00:02:47,789 --> 00:02:51,500
We like to imagine that if you were ill in the past, you

28
00:02:51,501 --> 00:02:56,637
might just wear an amulet or take a couple of magic potions or just pray to the gods.

29
00:02:56,638 --> 00:02:57,338
And to

30
00:02:57,339 --> 00:03:01,400
be fair, if you landed up in hospital in medieval Britain, that would be a reasonable description

31
00:03:01,401 --> 00:03:03,066
of what might happen.

32
00:03:03,067 --> 00:03:08,700
But the classical world is a completely different story and a much more

33
00:03:08,701 --> 00:03:10,089
sophisticated story.

34
00:03:10,090 --> 00:03:14,740
Many modern medical and surgical practices were not invented by us,

35
00:03:14,741 --> 00:03:16,620
they were reinvented by us.

36
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:25,460
The doctors and scientists working in medical theaters like this one in the early 19th century

37
00:03:25,461 --> 00:03:30,260
were the pioneers in their day, living in the great age of medical discovery.

38
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:36,300
But perhaps some of their ideas were not as revolutionary as history books have led us to believe.

39
00:03:44,740 --> 00:03:53,120
Two thousand years earlier, Greek and Roman doctors were carrying out operations, including

40
00:03:53,121 --> 00:04:01,420
eye surgery, bladder operations, fusing broken bones, and even a type of brain surgery.

41
00:04:01,421 --> 00:04:06,700
The most highly skilled of all these ancient surgeons was an extraordinary doctor and

42
00:04:06,701 --> 00:04:12,380
philosopher whose invention and ambition drove him to become the greatest medical expert

43
00:04:12,381 --> 00:04:14,220
of the Roman era.

44
00:04:14,221 --> 00:04:17,820
His name was Galen of Pergamum.

45
00:04:17,821 --> 00:04:24,900
Galen was a genius, arrogant, he's learned, he's observant.

46
00:04:24,901 --> 00:04:29,980
All these things tell you about somebody from the past in an immediate way that's very,

47
00:04:29,981 --> 00:04:34,460
very rare.

48
00:04:34,461 --> 00:04:39,160
Galen lived in an age when Roman dominion stretched from Europe to North Africa and

49
00:04:39,161 --> 00:04:40,900
the Middle East.

50
00:04:40,901 --> 00:04:46,580
His home was in Pergamum in modern-day Turkey, a civilized and wealthy Roman city in the

51
00:04:46,581 --> 00:04:50,880
eastern reaches of that great empire.

52
00:04:50,881 --> 00:04:56,960
Galen's father, a cultured architect, claimed he was told by the god Asclepius in a dream

53
00:04:56,961 --> 00:05:01,900
that his son should become a doctor, and there were few better places on earth for Galen

54
00:05:01,901 --> 00:05:10,420
to learn.

55
00:05:10,421 --> 00:05:15,120
Bergamom was a center for science, invention, and medicine.

56
00:05:15,121 --> 00:05:20,020
The perfect place to begin his extraordinary career.

57
00:05:20,021 --> 00:05:25,020
Only one place could teach him more, Alexandria in Egypt.

58
00:05:25,021 --> 00:05:39,021
When Galen was just 19 years old, fate took a hand.

59
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:46,320
The sudden death of his father gave him an inheritance, which allowed him to travel.

60
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:53,060
So it was to Alexandria that he came, the home of all the knowledge of the ancient world.

61
00:05:55,020 --> 00:06:09,020
The famous library of Alexandria was the place where Galen found inspiration.

62
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:15,020
Years before, when the Greeks had ruled this city, their leaders had allowed scientists

63
00:06:15,021 --> 00:06:24,020
to dissect human bodies in the quest for knowledge, something that was taboo in Galen's day.

64
00:06:24,021 --> 00:06:34,760
Their incredible discoveries about anatomy were still kept in the library, and it was here among these books that Galen's fascination with the workings of the human

65
00:06:34,761 --> 00:06:35,480
body began.

66
00:06:35,481 --> 00:06:48,720
even in Galen's time the liberal Alexandrian still allowed the study of human remains such

67
00:06:48,721 --> 00:06:54,980
as skeletons something forbidden elsewhere and this would give the young aspiring surgeon his

68
00:06:54,981 --> 00:07:01,280
first great opportunity this was a time when little was known about how the body worked

69
00:07:01,281 --> 00:07:04,140
and few people even dared to examine it.

70
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:08,920
But in Alexandria, Galen could freely look and learn.

71
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:12,340
While most people trusted their fate to the gods,

72
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:15,600
Galen was determined to control life and death

73
00:07:15,601 --> 00:07:18,660
to become the greatest doctor who ever lived.

74
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:24,660
Bandages for wounds have been used since ancient times,

75
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:27,800
but the modern-day bandage was perfected in 1920.

76
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:31,020
Earl Dixon invented the bandage for his wife,

77
00:07:31,021 --> 00:07:34,120
who had many kitchen accidents and needed a quick wound dressing.

78
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:36,740
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

79
00:07:51,340 --> 00:07:55,960
In Roman times, if you were sick and lived anywhere within the Roman Empire,

80
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:58,460
you might have come to a place like this.

81
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:07,740
This is an Asclepian. It was like a modern day spa with areas to rest and be healed.

82
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:14,120
There were doctors here, but at the Asclepian healing was very much in the hands of the gods.

83
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:17,500
To be cured required praying to Asclepius.

84
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:23,140
A great figure shown with a staff and a mighty serpent.

85
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:40,340
Offerings were made to the god in the form of carvings of the part of your body which

86
00:08:40,341 --> 00:08:42,320
needed to be healed.

87
00:08:42,321 --> 00:08:48,440
All manner of stone carvings have been discovered from hands and feet to ears, eyes and even

88
00:08:48,441 --> 00:08:51,100
a breast.

89
00:08:51,101 --> 00:08:57,160
The god Asclepius may today have been forgotten, but his staff and snake have remained an enduring

90
00:08:57,161 --> 00:09:00,160
symbol of medicine which survives to this day.

91
00:09:01,420 --> 00:09:04,400
The surgeon needed all of his abilities.

92
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:06,920
He also needed the assistance of the gods.

93
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:14,160
And in this instance, he found that assistance by having his instrument manufacturer make

94
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:17,120
the terminal in the form of a serpent's head.

95
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:22,620
So both the healer and the patient who had belief in that healing deity had a very positive

96
00:09:22,621 --> 00:09:26,960
attitude to the operation, which was a particularly beneficial part of ancient surgery.

97
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:38,480
Following his training in the library of Alexandria,

98
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:40,440
Galen traveled to Pergamum

99
00:09:40,441 --> 00:09:42,940
to take up his first professional appointment

100
00:09:42,941 --> 00:09:44,620
as a sports doctor.

101
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:51,700
This was an era when the original Olympic Games

102
00:09:51,701 --> 00:09:52,980
were still being held,

103
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:56,080
and the chance to tend these top sportsmen

104
00:09:56,081 --> 00:10:00,760
offered him the perfect opportunity to study bodies in peak physical condition,

105
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,900
as well as to learn about the injuries they suffered.

106
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:17,680
Then, as today, typical injuries included sprains and torn ligaments.

107
00:10:17,940 --> 00:10:25,920
And Galen used surgery, massage, ointments, medicines, and rehabilitation programs to cure these.

108
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:34,080
So effectively did he use the knowledge he'd gained in Alexandria that he would later be called the father of sports medicine.

109
00:10:34,081 --> 00:10:37,080
Sport was an obsession in the Roman world.

110
00:10:37,081 --> 00:10:46,080
From the gruesome spectacle of the gladiators to the classical Greek games at Olympia, audiences demanded to see the very best from their athletes.

111
00:10:46,081 --> 00:10:54,080
In running, discus and javelin, the hopes of an entire city or nation might lie in the hands of one athlete.

112
00:10:54,081 --> 00:11:01,080
and failure was unthinkable.

113
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:05,840
To our eyes some of Galen's treatments may seem very strange,

114
00:11:05,841 --> 00:11:12,840
such as the one he recommends for fractures and dislocations.

115
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:20,400
The treatment involved a doctor laying out his patient on a bed

116
00:11:20,401 --> 00:11:24,080
called the Hippocratic bench, named after its inventor,

117
00:11:24,081 --> 00:11:29,080
the great Greek doctor Hippocrates.

118
00:11:29,081 --> 00:11:36,080
The bed was designed to stretch the patient's body.

119
00:11:36,081 --> 00:11:42,680
He was strapped into the device using soft leather bindings.

120
00:11:42,681 --> 00:11:45,720
His legs were then raised and stretched using a handle

121
00:11:45,721 --> 00:11:48,720
which pulled the bones, ligaments and tendons.

122
00:11:53,720 --> 00:11:56,720
It might look like a medieval torture instrument,

123
00:11:56,721 --> 00:12:01,720
but in fact it's not very different from traction beds used in modern hospitals today.

124
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:06,720
Instead of wood and leather, we use metal and plastics,

125
00:12:06,721 --> 00:12:08,720
but the theory is the same.

126
00:12:08,721 --> 00:12:12,720
Using tension to align broken or dislocated bones

127
00:12:12,721 --> 00:12:16,280
ensured they set straight and the athlete fully recovered.

128
00:12:18,460 --> 00:12:23,140
For those who could not afford Galen, a far worse fate awaited.

129
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,020
This skeleton from a poor Roman grave shows a typical broken leg bone,

130
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:30,760
which fused in an incorrect position.

131
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:36,660
Galen's patience would have been far more fortunate.

132
00:12:39,940 --> 00:12:41,600
Galen's work was impressive.

133
00:12:41,601 --> 00:12:49,420
so impressive that it brought him to the attention of the organizers of a very different sport, gladiatorial combat.

134
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:09,280
This brutal, bloody sport left many combatants wounded or crippled,

135
00:13:09,281 --> 00:13:14,280
Even a minor wound in the arena could be a death blow as infection set in.

136
00:13:14,281 --> 00:13:20,280
Galen would boast that in his first year as their doctor, none he treated would die.

137
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:28,280
His secret lay in how he treated wounds, cleaning them with sponges soaked in wine,

138
00:13:28,281 --> 00:13:32,280
an early use of what today we would call antiseptics.

139
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:38,280
Galen could also see that some of the vicious wounds his gladiators received

140
00:13:38,281 --> 00:13:40,320
wouldn't heal if simply bandaged.

141
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:43,000
In cases where the wound was wide

142
00:13:43,001 --> 00:13:45,640
or vital muscles and tendons had been severed,

143
00:13:45,980 --> 00:13:48,320
he intervened, performing operations

144
00:13:48,321 --> 00:13:51,280
to stitch the separated ends of tissue together.

145
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:57,360
To help in his work, Galen used and improved

146
00:13:57,361 --> 00:13:59,340
on the available surgical instruments,

147
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:03,920
some of which have survived almost unchanged to the modern day.

148
00:14:03,921 --> 00:14:12,920
So important were a Roman doctor's instruments, he might even have them carved on his tomb.

149
00:14:12,921 --> 00:14:22,920
In rare cases, some of the actual tools that would have been familiar to doctors like Galen

150
00:14:22,921 --> 00:14:29,920
have survived, such as this set found in 1996 in the grave of a doctor in a village outside

151
00:14:29,921 --> 00:14:31,447
Colchester in England.

152
00:14:31,448 --> 00:14:35,960
So important was this exquisite set to its owner that he even had

153
00:14:35,961 --> 00:14:38,060
them buried with him.

154
00:14:38,061 --> 00:14:44,960
From this find, it was possible to reproduce this doctor's instrument

155
00:14:44,961 --> 00:14:51,960
set, a beautifully handcrafted collection of tools that even a modern surgeon would recognize.

156
00:14:51,961 --> 00:14:57,600
Galen was even more particular.

157
00:14:57,601 --> 00:15:03,140
He insisted all his tools were made to his exact specification, using only a fine type

158
00:15:03,141 --> 00:15:08,000
of iron from the Roman town of Noricum, which could be forged into what was effectively the

159
00:15:08,001 --> 00:15:15,800
first surgical steel.

160
00:15:15,801 --> 00:15:22,800
The British Museum in London contains some of the finest examples of Roman medical instruments.

161
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:28,980
Ralph Jackson, curator of the Roman British collections, is gaining a new insight into

162
00:15:28,981 --> 00:15:34,700
the sophisticated world of ancient surgery by studying them.

163
00:15:36,840 --> 00:15:41,355
The craftsmanship really is unsurpassed until very recent times.

164
00:15:41,356 --> 00:15:43,260
The instruments of holding,

165
00:15:43,261 --> 00:15:50,500
which is a sharp hook, used primarily to hold back the edges of wounds or incisions, has two parts.

166
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:53,940
It has a decorative handle, and it has the functional end,

167
00:15:53,941 --> 00:16:00,140
which terminates in a very, very sharp, beautifully turned hook, which did the work.

168
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:08,360
By placing a set of modern tools next to the ancient ones, striking similarities appear.

169
00:16:08,361 --> 00:16:17,140
all that really separates these instruments is the materials they are made out of

170
00:16:17,141 --> 00:16:20,240
and of course nearly 2 000 years

171
00:16:20,241 --> 00:16:30,020
roman sergeant instruments are stunning they are really marvelous pieces of craftsmanship

172
00:16:30,021 --> 00:16:36,220
and yet you also know as you handle them that they have been inside people's bodies performing

173
00:16:36,221 --> 00:16:41,740
operations, operations that are described by Galen and the ancient writers, the instruments

174
00:16:41,741 --> 00:16:44,100
themselves could not have been bettered.

175
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:50,500
But Galen, now in his early 30s, was about to pack up his instruments and move on.

176
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:55,340
His fame was spreading beyond the limited horizons of his hometown of Pergamum.

177
00:16:55,341 --> 00:16:59,380
He went to Rome in search of fame and fortune.

178
00:16:59,381 --> 00:17:07,000
In 1849, Elizabeth Blackwell became the first American woman to graduate from medical school.

179
00:17:07,660 --> 00:17:12,300
Her application to Geneva Medical College in New York was thought to be a practical joke.

180
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:17,420
Her fellow students were horrified when they realized their classmate was indeed a woman.

181
00:17:18,160 --> 00:17:19,920
She graduated first in her class.

182
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:22,240
Modern Marvels will continue in a moment.

183
00:17:29,380 --> 00:17:35,980
When Galen arrived in Rome, it was the busiest city in the world.

184
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:38,800
Everything here was bigger and better,

185
00:17:38,801 --> 00:17:41,080
from the wealth of his private clients

186
00:17:41,081 --> 00:17:44,660
to the bloody excesses of Rome's own gladiatorial games

187
00:17:44,661 --> 00:17:47,420
in the vast amphitheater of the Colosseum.

188
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:51,680
Rome's gladiators were the empire's finest,

189
00:17:52,380 --> 00:17:54,520
the superstar sportsmen of their day.

190
00:17:55,340 --> 00:17:58,920
Saving their lives could literally save their owners a fortune.

191
00:17:59,380 --> 00:18:05,200
But healing gladiators didn't just bring Galen money.

192
00:18:05,201 --> 00:18:10,320
In a world where fighting to the death was allowed, but dissecting humans was banned,

193
00:18:10,321 --> 00:18:14,320
the terrible injuries they sustained gave him the chance to glimpse the inner workings

194
00:18:14,321 --> 00:18:16,280
of the human body.

195
00:18:16,281 --> 00:18:21,180
While these men brutally dissected each other, Galen could watch and learn.

196
00:18:52,234 --> 00:18:58,500
Where other doctors appealed to gods or resorted to potions that were little more than folk magic,

197
00:18:58,501 --> 00:19:02,180
Galen observed his patients, examining their symptoms,

198
00:19:02,620 --> 00:19:05,340
diagnosed the problem, and suggested a cure.

199
00:19:05,820 --> 00:19:10,000
His methodical treatment of the human body as a machine was revolutionary

200
00:19:10,001 --> 00:19:14,200
and forms the basic technique for medical inquiry to this day.

201
00:19:25,340 --> 00:19:28,440
To add to the grisly revelations of the Games,

202
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:32,740
Galen did dissect other animals to explore their inner workings

203
00:19:32,741 --> 00:19:35,320
in the hope it would shed light on the human body.

204
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:42,060
These experiments, often carried out in public and on live animals,

205
00:19:42,580 --> 00:19:45,080
even caused uneasiness in his own day.

206
00:19:48,280 --> 00:19:50,940
But these were more than gruesome shows.

207
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:54,360
By tying threads around nerves in the animal's neck,

208
00:19:54,361 --> 00:19:56,940
he showed how he could stop animals moving

209
00:19:56,941 --> 00:19:58,780
or making a noise at will.

210
00:19:59,320 --> 00:20:01,540
Even in these distressing displays,

211
00:20:02,040 --> 00:20:05,600
Galen was laying down the ground rules for neurosurgery.

212
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:26,620
Galen's brilliant reputation and flair for showmanship

213
00:20:26,621 --> 00:20:30,440
eventually brought him to the attention of the greatest Roman of them all,

214
00:20:30,940 --> 00:20:32,900
the Emperor Marcus Aurelius.

215
00:20:41,780 --> 00:20:47,620
From now on, Galen would be entrusted with the health of the first family in the ancient world

216
00:20:47,621 --> 00:20:50,340
as court physician to the Emperor.

217
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:54,420
It was the pinnacle of any doctor's ambition,

218
00:20:54,421 --> 00:20:58,000
but it was not without its own very real risks.

219
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:01,680
Ensuring the health of the most powerful man on earth,

220
00:21:02,160 --> 00:21:04,940
a man with enemies, was no easy matter.

221
00:21:06,580 --> 00:21:09,280
And when the emperor's young son fell ill,

222
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:13,860
Galen may have felt it was not just the boy's life that was at risk.

223
00:21:18,160 --> 00:21:20,100
But his treatment was successful,

224
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:23,120
and Galen would be kept close by the emperor

225
00:21:23,121 --> 00:21:24,740
for the rest of his reign.

226
00:21:28,640 --> 00:21:30,800
Galen, always the self-publicist,

227
00:21:31,180 --> 00:21:33,360
wrote tirelessly on medical subjects,

228
00:21:33,920 --> 00:21:35,320
compiling hundreds of books

229
00:21:35,321 --> 00:21:37,960
to spread his techniques across the empire.

230
00:21:39,700 --> 00:21:43,400
And as Galen prospered, Roman medicine flourished,

231
00:21:43,401 --> 00:21:46,580
as witnessed by the huge numbers of medical instruments

232
00:21:46,581 --> 00:21:48,380
that survived from this time.

233
00:21:48,381 --> 00:21:55,380
One such instrument is this perfectly engineered speculum, which was discovered in Pompeii.

234
00:21:56,380 --> 00:21:59,461
It may have been used during childbirth or surgery.

235
00:21:59,462 --> 00:22:05,380
It may be made of bronze, but it would have been just as effective as a similar device used today.

236
00:22:05,381 --> 00:22:11,660
used today.

237
00:22:11,661 --> 00:22:17,620
As a physician to the emperor, Galen's services were now in demand among the wealthiest citizens,

238
00:22:17,621 --> 00:22:25,340
such as the inhabitants of the prosperous town of Ostia Antica outside Rome.

239
00:22:25,341 --> 00:22:30,720
Away from the blood of the amphitheater or the intrigues of court, these people had different

240
00:22:30,721 --> 00:22:34,000
health priorities.

241
00:22:34,001 --> 00:22:40,320
Their town had sports stadiums, baths, and a gymnasium where Galen treated everyday injuries.

242
00:22:42,460 --> 00:22:46,240
It also boasted the latest in plumbing and sewage disposal,

243
00:22:47,020 --> 00:22:50,500
all built into the fabric of its public buildings and private homes.

244
00:22:51,160 --> 00:22:56,940
This was a town where hygiene and health, not life and death, were the priorities.

245
00:22:56,941 --> 00:23:05,720
While most of the world's population worried about keeping alive, the wealthy people here

246
00:23:05,721 --> 00:23:16,440
could worry about just staying in perfect condition.

247
00:23:16,441 --> 00:23:23,840
They wanted cosmetic surgery, and doctors like Galen could provide it.

248
00:23:23,841 --> 00:23:27,340
curse of the Roman aristocracy was bad teeth.

249
00:23:27,341 --> 00:23:30,840
While most peasants eating only a simple diet

250
00:23:30,841 --> 00:23:38,021
had good teeth, Romans fed on luxury did not. The solution was false teeth.

251
00:23:38,022 --> 00:23:39,840
Originally invented

252
00:23:40,040 --> 00:23:44,920
by the Etruscans, they were also known to have been developed by the Romans and made

253
00:23:44,921 --> 00:23:51,440
from either human or animal teeth, or sometimes ivory or bone, and held together by a band

254
00:23:51,441 --> 00:23:58,440
of gold. Many of the techniques used were similar to dental techniques of today.

255
00:24:04,320 --> 00:24:05,020
Dr.

256
00:24:05,021 --> 00:24:10,040
Paola Catalano from Rome's archaeological service made one such discovery when she was

257
00:24:10,041 --> 00:24:13,377
working on a site in a mausoleum in Rome.

258
00:24:13,378 --> 00:24:17,040
This is the only example of Roman false teeth

259
00:24:17,041 --> 00:24:24,040
This is the only example of Roman false teeth ever found from the first century.

260
00:24:41,454 --> 00:24:50,300
The teeth are held together using fine gold wire, and amazingly threads of silk can still be seen which were bound around the teeth.

261
00:24:54,040 --> 00:24:57,040
that Romans, in imperial time,

262
00:24:57,041 --> 00:25:00,040
did this kind of interventions,

263
00:25:00,041 --> 00:25:05,040
even if it was the only case that we had found.

264
00:25:08,040 --> 00:25:11,040
Other types of cosmetic surgery were also in use.

265
00:25:18,040 --> 00:25:21,040
Ancient doctors could remove tattoos,

266
00:25:21,041 --> 00:25:28,340
They could repair damaged earlobes where an earring or a mark of slavery had been torn from the ear.

267
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:38,640
They even practiced breast reductions, though one author says not for cosmetic purposes.

268
00:25:38,641 --> 00:25:49,560
Perhaps Galen himself removed slavery tattoos in the dark vaults of the Coliseum after a

269
00:25:49,561 --> 00:25:54,660
successful gladiator won his freedom in the arena.

270
00:25:54,661 --> 00:26:00,420
To help with the inevitable pain of such operations, Galen wrote of the sedative effects of opium

271
00:26:00,421 --> 00:26:02,960
and alcohol on patients.

272
00:26:02,961 --> 00:26:08,460
In many cases, however, sheer willpower was all that was available.

273
00:26:08,461 --> 00:26:13,020
It was now that Galen would attempt the most difficult and challenging operation he had

274
00:26:13,021 --> 00:26:15,860
yet undertaken, eye surgery.

275
00:26:15,861 --> 00:26:28,455
The first human heart transplant was performed on December 3, 1967 by Dr.

276
00:26:28,456 --> 00:26:31,560
Christian Barnard.

277
00:26:31,561 --> 00:26:35,320
The 55-year-old recipient lived for 18 days.

278
00:26:35,321 --> 00:26:36,900
Marvels will continue in a moment.

279
00:26:44,520 --> 00:26:49,560
Willpower and a steady hand were always called for in the case of one of the most incredible

280
00:26:49,561 --> 00:26:54,218
operations carried out during the Roman period, eye surgery.

281
00:26:54,219 --> 00:26:56,780
Romans like us today could suffer

282
00:26:56,781 --> 00:27:01,547
from cataracts, a clouding of the lens in the eye that can lead to blindness.

283
00:27:01,548 --> 00:27:02,600
But also like us,

284
00:27:02,601 --> 00:27:05,940
they had a cure, a delicate operation.

285
00:27:06,940 --> 00:27:08,620
Ketak operations seem to be common.

286
00:27:09,660 --> 00:27:16,440
It's a very simple operation in which you put a very thin needle into the eye and flip over the lens.

287
00:27:17,040 --> 00:27:21,620
And we've got lots of instruments, mainly from France but elsewhere in the Roman Empire,

288
00:27:22,040 --> 00:27:24,860
that seem to show that this was a common procedure.

289
00:27:24,861 --> 00:27:38,861
The technique known as couching involved the use of a very fine needle.

290
00:27:40,320 --> 00:27:47,920
Surgeons would expertly insert the needle into the back of the eye to remove the cataract.

291
00:27:47,921 --> 00:27:54,680
One slip could have caused permanent blindness.

292
00:27:54,681 --> 00:27:59,440
In some developing countries today, the technique of couching is carried out in exactly the

293
00:27:59,441 --> 00:28:05,980
same way as it would have been by Roman doctors.

294
00:28:05,981 --> 00:28:15,220
A unique find in Montbele, France, has revealed an even more advanced technique in ancient

295
00:28:15,221 --> 00:28:17,680
eye surgery.

296
00:28:17,681 --> 00:28:22,560
These instruments were found in a bronze tube on the bed of the river Seine, so they're

297
00:28:22,561 --> 00:28:30,620
very well preserved and as well as three solid cataract couching needles there are two needle

298
00:28:30,621 --> 00:28:36,436
syringes. These amazingly delicate syringes were at first a mystery.

299
00:28:36,437 --> 00:28:38,660
It was only when the tools

300
00:28:38,661 --> 00:28:41,826
were x-rayed that their secret was revealed.

301
00:28:41,827 --> 00:28:44,920
Using these radiographs modern eye surgeons

302
00:28:44,921 --> 00:28:47,980
could then begin to explain what they were for.

303
00:28:52,560 --> 00:28:57,680
This is an even more sophisticated instrument,

304
00:28:57,681 --> 00:29:00,480
and it's a very cunning piece of design,

305
00:29:00,900 --> 00:29:07,440
in that it's a hollow instrument with an introducer.

306
00:29:08,040 --> 00:29:11,460
So with this, it would be a more sophisticated operation

307
00:29:11,461 --> 00:29:14,440
of being able to enter the lens itself,

308
00:29:15,760 --> 00:29:20,620
and then the surgeon would remove the needle from inside

309
00:29:20,621 --> 00:29:28,540
and be able to suck the lens contents out through the small port.

310
00:29:34,660 --> 00:29:39,560
But for Galen, performing delicate operations on the rich and famous in Rome

311
00:29:39,561 --> 00:29:41,980
was not to be the end of his career.

312
00:29:42,840 --> 00:29:47,640
Emperor Marcus Aurelius knew there was only one body more important than his own,

313
00:29:48,240 --> 00:29:49,820
the body of the army.

314
00:29:50,620 --> 00:29:55,620
Marcus Aurelius wanted Galen to go to war.

315
00:29:57,720 --> 00:30:01,020
It was the Roman army that held the empire together,

316
00:30:01,480 --> 00:30:07,060
and so it was vital that as many of its soldiers were as fit as possible as often as possible.

317
00:30:07,720 --> 00:30:10,840
If they were healthy, then the empire was healthy.

318
00:30:11,540 --> 00:30:15,320
And if the empire was healthy, then Marcus Aurelius was happy.

319
00:30:16,200 --> 00:30:21,400
The one group in the Roman Empire whose health matters more than any other is, of course, the army.

320
00:30:22,680 --> 00:30:30,800
The army and its fitness and its health and its ability to recover the strength and prosperity of the empire depended.

321
00:30:30,801 --> 00:30:39,880
It's not surprising, then, that the Roman emperor was interested that Galen should turn his skills and resources towards helping the army

322
00:30:39,881 --> 00:30:44,920
and investigate how quickly best to mend fractures, to heal wounds,

323
00:30:45,140 --> 00:30:46,860
to keep the army as fit as possible.

324
00:30:49,820 --> 00:30:53,460
Of course, treating soldiers was very different from treating civilians.

325
00:30:54,420 --> 00:30:57,340
Most doctors would never dare cut into the body of a civilian

326
00:30:57,341 --> 00:30:59,020
because of the risk of infection.

327
00:30:59,940 --> 00:31:03,900
But with soldiers, the enemy had often opened up the body for them.

328
00:31:03,901 --> 00:31:16,140
Saving these soldiers was the key to military medicine.

329
00:31:16,141 --> 00:31:22,860
Having spent years treating gladiators, Gila knew all about wounds and the risks of infection.

330
00:31:22,861 --> 00:31:27,080
And he knew the best place to cure them was in a hospital.

331
00:31:27,081 --> 00:31:32,060
These were the first true hospitals in the ancient world, specialist centers for the

332
00:31:32,061 --> 00:31:33,505
treatment of soldiers.

333
00:31:33,506 --> 00:31:38,300
Walk into one and you would have walked into a surprisingly modern world.

334
00:31:39,740 --> 00:31:43,411
Through the gatehouse there was a light and spacious ward.

335
00:31:43,412 --> 00:31:45,500
Beyond was the operating theater,

336
00:31:45,501 --> 00:31:50,620
projecting out into the courtyard to allow the maximum amount of light for the surgeons.

337
00:32:02,060 --> 00:32:07,760
In one corner stood a complex of baths with a block of flushing toilets attached.

338
00:32:08,700 --> 00:32:11,400
Hygiene was very important to doctors like Galen.

339
00:32:13,160 --> 00:32:20,460
In the other corner stood the kitchens, where the healthy, balanced diets required by convalescing patients were prepared.

340
00:32:22,740 --> 00:32:29,160
In the other wings lay a series of small wards designed to reduce the risk of cross-infection.

341
00:32:32,060 --> 00:32:40,880
There were also consulting rooms, a dispensary, and for the unfortunates that not even the

342
00:32:40,881 --> 00:32:45,780
great Galen could save, a mortuary.

343
00:32:45,781 --> 00:32:50,900
The Roman hospital really was an incredibly modern institution.

344
00:32:50,901 --> 00:32:55,340
And it's not just in the layout of the building, but the whole approach to how patients were

345
00:32:55,341 --> 00:32:58,840
treated.

346
00:32:58,841 --> 00:33:03,280
to people like gaylin a soldier in the roman army was more likely to survive

347
00:33:03,281 --> 00:33:09,580
conflict than at any other time up until the first world war some wounded soldiers

348
00:33:09,581 --> 00:33:13,300
may even have had the benefit of modern sterile surgery

349
00:33:13,301 --> 00:33:25,480
in an operating theater at a hospital in nice raised hearts were found these were probably

350
00:33:25,481 --> 00:33:31,720
where the surgical tools were sterilized in the fire before use, just as Galen suggested.

351
00:33:40,080 --> 00:33:45,640
As the years passed, Galen was slowly putting the jigsaw of human anatomy together,

352
00:33:46,200 --> 00:33:50,780
thanks to his work in military hospitals and in his own private experiments.

353
00:33:51,620 --> 00:33:54,580
By examining the organs of humans and animals,

354
00:33:55,080 --> 00:33:57,480
their different functions were becoming clear to him,

355
00:33:58,120 --> 00:33:59,800
something he knew was essential

356
00:33:59,801 --> 00:34:03,600
if he was to truly understand how the human body works.

357
00:34:13,540 --> 00:34:16,660
Galen believed that in order to understand medicine,

358
00:34:16,920 --> 00:34:18,360
you had to know anatomy.

359
00:34:19,060 --> 00:34:25,400
He himself worked almost entirely on animals, though as a surgeon he'd cut up human bodies.

360
00:34:27,220 --> 00:34:30,400
He carried out experiments on the nervous system.

361
00:34:30,560 --> 00:34:37,140
He believed you needed to know how the body was organized in order to be a good doctor.

362
00:34:41,280 --> 00:34:46,900
Most remarkably, Galen made the connection between the brain and the nervous system.

363
00:34:47,740 --> 00:34:50,940
He knew, for example, that a problem with paralysis of the hand

364
00:34:50,941 --> 00:34:54,020
might be treated by manipulating the spinal cord,

365
00:34:54,480 --> 00:34:59,480
and that a symptom in one place might be caused by a problem somewhere quite different.

366
00:35:00,160 --> 00:35:02,320
That was Galen's great difference.

367
00:35:02,321 --> 00:35:08,340
He identified and treated the problem, not just the symptom, as most doctors did.

368
00:35:08,341 --> 00:35:18,780
nowhere was this more important than in treating head injuries incurred in battle or the arena

369
00:35:18,781 --> 00:35:25,740
galen realized the brain controlled the whole body he was such an accomplished surgeon he

370
00:35:25,741 --> 00:35:30,300
attempted the impossible surgery on the skull and brain

371
00:35:30,301 --> 00:35:39,140
The first contact lenses were made in the late 1800s by a German physiologist.

372
00:35:39,141 --> 00:35:44,160
They were made from heavy brown glass and were 18 to 21 millimeters in diameter.

373
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:46,740
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

374
00:35:56,260 --> 00:35:59,720
Blows to the head might produce symptoms all over the body.

375
00:35:59,721 --> 00:36:01,780
from paralysis to pain.

376
00:36:02,440 --> 00:36:06,360
But Galen realized the key was to treat the problem area on the skull.

377
00:36:07,620 --> 00:36:10,760
To do this, a section of skull would have to be removed

378
00:36:10,761 --> 00:36:12,020
to relieve the pressure

379
00:36:12,021 --> 00:36:15,400
and remove any bone splinters pressing on the brain,

380
00:36:15,640 --> 00:36:17,620
a process called trepanning.

381
00:36:18,440 --> 00:36:21,440
There is evidence for crude forms of this procedure,

382
00:36:21,940 --> 00:36:23,560
dating back into prehistory,

383
00:36:24,140 --> 00:36:28,160
although we don't know if it was performed for medical or magical purposes.

384
00:36:29,720 --> 00:36:37,720
But until recently, it was assumed that this operation could only have been performed successfully by modern surgeons,

385
00:36:37,721 --> 00:36:44,720
as the risk was infection or damage to the delicate structures of the brain would have made ancient attempts impossible.

386
00:36:53,720 --> 00:36:57,720
But then an extraordinary series of skulls began to come to light.

387
00:36:57,721 --> 00:36:58,421
to light.

388
00:36:58,422 --> 00:37:04,400
In the History of Medicine Museum at La Sapienza University in Rome, one skull

389
00:37:04,401 --> 00:37:09,000
in particular tells an amazing story.

390
00:37:09,001 --> 00:37:16,488
This is the skull of a five-year-old child who underwent trepanning.

391
00:37:16,489 --> 00:37:18,360
From the front it

392
00:37:18,361 --> 00:37:24,240
appears perfectly normal, but on the top is what looks like a terrible injury, a large

393
00:37:24,241 --> 00:37:31,240
hole cut in the cranium about six centimeters across.

394
00:37:31,241 --> 00:37:36,280
But a closer examination revealed that the bone had been deliberately cut and that the

395
00:37:36,281 --> 00:37:44,500
child had survived after the operation.

396
00:37:44,501 --> 00:37:49,800
Earlier skulls had shown signs of deliberate removal of bone from fractures, but no one

397
00:37:49,801 --> 00:37:52,220
had seen anything like this child's skull.

398
00:38:00,100 --> 00:38:03,760
Dr. Paolo Catalano, from Rome's archaeological service,

399
00:38:04,300 --> 00:38:06,880
discovered this skull dated from the time of Galen.

400
00:38:19,800 --> 00:38:24,580
and was found in a necropolis of the second century after C,

401
00:38:25,300 --> 00:38:28,380
excavated a few years ago in Fidene,

402
00:38:28,500 --> 00:38:31,720
in the area of the suburbia north of Rome.

403
00:38:32,620 --> 00:38:35,780
The procedure on the skull closely resembled one

404
00:38:35,781 --> 00:38:39,160
recommended by Galen himself for some brain diseases.

405
00:38:40,180 --> 00:38:42,900
Perhaps this was the work of the master himself.

406
00:38:44,040 --> 00:38:46,400
This characteristic of the chronic lesion,

407
00:38:46,401 --> 00:38:53,400
and it was then examined with quite advanced techniques

408
00:38:53,401 --> 00:38:58,400
and has also shown, in addition to this study,

409
00:38:58,401 --> 00:39:03,400
that the technique followed precisely the details of Galeno.

410
00:39:03,401 --> 00:39:06,400
in a precise manner, the galen's dettami.

411
00:39:13,520 --> 00:39:18,120
Galen explained how he had observed brain damage occurring in patients who had complaints

412
00:39:18,121 --> 00:39:23,580
such as inflammation of the brain and recorded how this might be avoided by releasing the

413
00:39:23,581 --> 00:39:25,280
pressure using trepanation.

414
00:39:33,400 --> 00:39:38,220
This is what appears to have happened in the case of the young child.

415
00:39:42,360 --> 00:39:52,420
This case, the trepanation, was performed because of an expansive disease of the right cerebral hemisphere.

416
00:39:52,880 --> 00:40:01,640
So the surgeon at one point operated on this skull in order to relieve the pressure that developed inside the skull.

417
00:40:01,641 --> 00:40:06,340
and the child survived for a short period of time.

418
00:40:09,420 --> 00:40:13,040
This was not simply an attempt to remove damaged bone,

419
00:40:13,640 --> 00:40:16,920
but closer to being a form of ancient brain surgery.

420
00:40:19,620 --> 00:40:23,260
What is remarkable about this ancient cranial surgery

421
00:40:23,261 --> 00:40:28,160
is that it was performed to treat a disease of the brain

422
00:40:28,161 --> 00:40:33,620
and not to remove a crack or damage the area of the bone itself.

423
00:40:34,140 --> 00:40:36,680
And this is what is amazing about this find.

424
00:40:38,320 --> 00:40:42,820
Who at the time this child lived would have had the knowledge and skill

425
00:40:42,821 --> 00:40:46,840
to perform this complex operation other than Galen himself?

426
00:40:52,880 --> 00:40:57,160
We know much about Galen, as he was a great self-publicist

427
00:40:57,161 --> 00:41:02,560
who liked to write at least three pages a day and left hundreds of books to posterity.

428
00:41:11,400 --> 00:41:13,760
But this would prove a bitter legacy.

429
00:41:14,640 --> 00:41:18,240
His work was so revolutionary and his writing so prolific

430
00:41:18,241 --> 00:41:22,500
that when he died, other doctors took his work as being definitive

431
00:41:22,501 --> 00:41:25,240
and failed to continue his research.

432
00:41:25,241 --> 00:41:29,051
Galen was the architect of his own failure.

433
00:41:29,052 --> 00:41:33,040
Subsequent generations couldn't cope with the

434
00:41:33,041 --> 00:41:39,880
amount he'd written, so they produced summaries, handbooks, giving what they thought was the major

435
00:41:39,881 --> 00:41:45,700
summaries of his ideas. What they left out was what makes Galen impressive today.

436
00:41:45,701 --> 00:41:51,980
Even up until the 16th century, Galen's work was being used as reference,

437
00:41:51,981 --> 00:41:57,840
and from Galen's death to that time, hardly any further advances were made in Western medicine.

438
00:42:15,060 --> 00:42:20,500
Only in the last 500 years have anatomists critically examined his work

439
00:42:20,501 --> 00:42:23,160
and realized how much he had misunderstood.

440
00:42:27,820 --> 00:42:30,520
With only limited access to human bodies,

441
00:42:31,140 --> 00:42:34,760
Galen had based many of his theories on the study of animal anatomy,

442
00:42:35,300 --> 00:42:38,180
which later doctors found to be very different from our own.

443
00:42:39,820 --> 00:42:44,040
Galen has therefore been disregarded and dismissed for hundreds of years.

444
00:42:44,600 --> 00:42:47,480
It's only recently that we have started to look back

445
00:42:47,481 --> 00:42:50,340
and realize how much he actually got right.

446
00:42:50,500 --> 00:42:57,540
and how close he had come to explaining the mysteries of the workings of the most complex machine of all, the human body.

447
00:42:58,140 --> 00:43:02,080
I think Galen is underrated and deserves a better press.

448
00:43:02,300 --> 00:43:06,500
He's important and very modern in his concern in the practice of medicine,

449
00:43:06,900 --> 00:43:13,260
not only in a sense about healing people, their wounds and diseases, but also treating them as a complete patient.

450
00:43:13,900 --> 00:43:18,200
He inspires the modern medicine of treating the whole person.

451
00:43:20,500 --> 00:43:27,500
William Harvey, the man who discovered the circulation of the blood in the 1700s, said

452
00:43:29,000 --> 00:43:34,214
Galen came so close to making the same discovery all those years earlier.

453
00:43:34,215 --> 00:43:36,000
But when Galen eventually

454
00:43:38,400 --> 00:43:45,400
died at the age of 87, the heroic age of the Roman Empire was already coming to an end,

455
00:43:45,401 --> 00:43:49,160
and with it the great age of medical discovery.

456
00:43:49,161 --> 00:43:52,120
He had been a man so ahead of his time

457
00:43:52,121 --> 00:43:55,340
that for centuries no one would question him.

458
00:43:55,341 --> 00:44:00,900
Then for centuries more, no one would believe him.

459
00:44:00,901 --> 00:44:03,280
Now we can perhaps finally look back

460
00:44:03,281 --> 00:44:06,500
and appreciate the amazing discoveries that he made

461
00:44:06,501 --> 00:44:09,320
and admire the audacity and brilliance of a man

462
00:44:09,321 --> 00:44:11,960
who would attempt operations not seen again

463
00:44:11,961 --> 00:44:14,820
anywhere on earth for nearly two millennia.

464
00:44:15,460 --> 00:44:20,160
A man who laid the foundations of modern medicine in the ancient world.

465
00:44:22,080 --> 00:44:26,380
